  "rationales": {
    "wacc": "10.5% (analyst judgment). AMD's capital structure is almost all equity: long-term debt $7.1B (balance sheet, 2026-09-26) against a market capitalisation of roughly $790B (last close $483.90 on 2026-11-03 x 1,640M cover-page shares), so WACC is roughly the cost of equity. Cost of equity assumes a risk-free rate of about 4.25%, an equity risk premium of about 5% and a beta of about 1.25 for a high-beta, cyclical semiconductor designer with concentrated customers (periodic.txt notes a small number of customers account for a substantial part of revenue) and export-control exposure (Q-6). That gives about 10.5%. The cost of debt is not material at this weight. The risk-free rate, ERP and beta are not in the snapshot; they are assumptions, not sourced facts.",
    "terminal_growth": "3.0% (analyst judgment): roughly long-run nominal GDP plus a modest premium for semiconductor content growth (AI/data-center compute). It stays well below the 10.5% WACC and does not assume the current AI-driven growth continues forever.",
    "revenue_growth": "Year 1 is the code's NTM revenue from assumptions.json (about $72.6B, anchored on the $17.0B FY2026Q4 guidance midpoint, Q-1). That is already about 60% above TTM revenue of $45.4B (FY2025Q4 $10.27B + FY2026Q1-Q3 $35.17B; F-da2eaa33df less F-24d3538948, plus F-80df404447). Years 2-5 slow down: 25%, 18%, 12%, 8%. The data-center/rack-scale ramp (Q-3, Q-4) supports strong growth after year 1, but a base this large, lumpy system shipments and export-control risk (Q-6) argue for a glide path towards the terminal rate rather than a straight extrapolation of the FY2026 trend. These rates are analyst judgment.",
    "peers": "NVDA, AVGO, MRVL, INTC: the coverage-list names whose business is closest to AMD's. NVDA competes directly in data-center GPUs/accelerators. AVGO and MRVL sell data-center compute and networking silicon (custom accelerators) to the same hyperscale customers. INTC competes directly with AMD in x86 client and server CPUs, AMD's other large franchise. Excluded: MU (commodity memory with a different margin cycle); AMAT, LRCX and KLAC (wafer-fab equipment, a capex-cycle business model); SNPS and CDNS (EDA software); ALAB and CRDO (small-cap connectivity names with TTM revenue near $1.6B, where scale and growth profile are not comparable); MPWR (power management). Including INTC (a low EV/Sales name) alongside the AI-premium names keeps the median representative of AMD's mixed CPU-plus-accelerator portfolio. Pre-check of TTM EV/Sales from the snapshot: NVDA ~16.5x, AVGO ~18.2x, MRVL ~16.1x, INTC ~5.8x; code computes the final median.",
    "fcf_margin": "Historical FCF margin = (CFO - capex) / revenue, all from snapshot XBRL facts. FY2022 13.2% ((3,565-450)/23,601); FY2023 4.9% ((1,667-546)/22,680); FY2024 9.3% ((3,041-636)/25,785); FY2025 19.4% ((7,709-974)/34,639; F-ba5e841822, F-9407f71787, F-da2eaa33df); 9M FY2026 13.3% ((6,523-1,845)/35,166; F-453f71f069, F-15168cdccc, F-80df404447); TTM to 2026-09-26 15.5% ((7,056)/45,436). Quarterly FY2026 runs 25.0%, 13.5%, 4.1% (Q1-Q3). The decline reflects a working-capital build (inventories $7.9B to $11.5B, receivables $6.3B to $9.2B since 2025-12-27) and capex up to about 5% of revenue. Year 1 at 18% sits between TTM (15.5%) and the FY2025 peak (19.4%): the model's GAAP operating margin of about 25-26% (assumptions.json gross margin ~53.5% less opex ~28%) supports more than TTM, but 60% revenue growth will keep absorbing working capital. Margins rise to 20%, 21% and 22% as growth slows, working-capital drag eases and fixed intangible amortization/opex shrink relative to revenue, then hold at 22%. That is still below the after-tax operating margin plus non-cash add-backs, as a haircut for capex intensity and because CFO includes the SBC add-back (SBC ~4.3% of 9M FY2026 revenue, F-c00e259756), which is a real economic cost.",
    "dcf_weight": "0.6 DCF / 0.4 peer EV/Sales. The DCF carries more weight because it rests on this note's explicit, sourced forecasts (guidance-anchored NTM revenue, history-grounded FCF margins). The peer EV/Sales cross-check is applied to AMD's TTM revenue. It gets real weight because it captures the market's sector-wide pricing of AI-compute growth beyond our five-year horizon, which a fixed-margin DCF may understate. It does not get a majority because those multiples embed peer-specific growth and margin profiles (NVDA's much higher margins, INTC's restructuring) and current sentiment rather than AMD's cash flows."
